WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.617 --> 00:00:04.540
This arrow is a qubit.

2
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.847
Pointing straight up, it means zero.

3
00:00:06.847 --> 00:00:08.001
Straight down, one.

4
00:00:08.001 --> 00:00:10.694
But it can point anywhere in between,

5
00:00:10.694 --> 00:00:14.540
and that in-between is where quantum computers get their power.

6
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:18.712
The trouble is that the arrow never holds still.

7
00:00:18.712 --> 00:00:22.558
Stray fields, heat, a control pulse meant for a neighbor:

8
00:00:22.558 --> 00:00:26.788
each one nudges it a little, and the nudges add up.

9
00:00:27.517 --> 00:00:31.363
On an ordinary computer we would fight this with backups.

10
00:00:31.363 --> 00:00:34.824
Copy the bit three times and take a vote.

11
00:00:34.824 --> 00:00:37.132
For qubits, that move is forbidden.

12
00:00:37.817 --> 00:00:44.355
Try to copy an unknown state with the obvious circuit, and you do not get two copies.

13
00:00:44.355 --> 00:00:46.663
You get two qubits tangled together,

14
00:00:46.663 --> 00:00:51.278
and their overlap with the copies you wanted is exactly one half.

15
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:53.873
This is the no-cloning theorem.

16
00:00:53.873 --> 00:00:57.335
Whatever protects a qubit, it cannot be a backup.
